      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coyote is named after nearby Coyote Canyon, itself named for the many coyotes that once roamed the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bobeckertphotography.com/coyote-new-mexico.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1808 homesteaders built a walled, fortified village in Coyote, complete with walls, corrals, fields and ditches.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bobeckertphotography.com/coyote-new-mexico.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village was abandoned when Utes raided it, forcing residents to flee back to Abiquí or Santa Cruz.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bobeckertphotography.com/coyote-new-mexico.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After Kit Carson’s Long Walk in 1846, homesteaders returned to Coyote and began building farmhouses.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bobeckertphotography.com/coyote-new-mexico.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1917 the communal land that had belonged to all settlers was transferred to the National Forest.",
